Chapter: LocationStub
  cites Foreword chapter Location

 Read the chapter name before the numbers. Nothing here consults
 hardware; there is no hardware to consult. Every answer below is a
 named constant, and the names say so, so that a value read out of a
 running program cannot be mistaken for a fix. A caller that needs to
 know whether it is holding a reading or a placeholder asks
 location-is-fixed, which answers False here and must answer True in any
 chapter that ever replaces this one over a real receiver.

 Why this exists at all rather than nothing. An unimplemented effect
 operation is not neutral: it type-checks and then dies at emit with
 CDX2040, so a program that merely MENTIONS locate cannot be built even
 if it never performs it. Constants let the shape of a program that uses
 location be written, compiled and tested now, on the standing decision
 to stub hardware we do not have until we have it. What they must never
 do is look like measurements, which is the whole reason for the naming
 above.

 The coordinates are the null island point, latitude zero and longitude
 zero, deliberately: it is in the Gulf of Guinea, it is the value that
 falls out of a zeroed struct in every GIS system, and it is therefore
 the one place on earth that reads as "this is not a position" to anyone
 who has handled coordinate data. A plausible-looking street address
 would be the dishonest choice.

 We say:

Section: Units

 Latitude and longitude are microdegrees, so a whole-number type carries
 roughly eleven centimetres of resolution and no rounding is hidden in a
 conversion. Altitude is metres above the WGS 84 ellipsoid.

  location-microdegrees-per-degree : Integer = 1000000

Section: The Fixed Answer

  LocationFix = record {
    lf-latitude : Integer,
    lf-longitude : Integer
  }

  location-stub-latitude : Integer = 0
  location-stub-longitude : Integer = 0
  location-stub-altitude : Integer = 0

  location-is-fixed : [Location] Boolean
  location-is-fixed = False

  locate : [Location] LocationFix
  locate = LocationFix {
    lf-latitude = location-stub-latitude,
    lf-longitude = location-stub-longitude
  }

  altitude : [Location] Integer
  altitude = location-stub-altitude

